/**
 * @ The Geology domain
 * 
 * Like the @atmosphere domain, @geology is a wide-spectrum namespace that provides basic articulation for several disciplines
 * explaining the @earth. These include physical and historical geologies, mineralogy and petrology, geophysics, sedimentology and
 * stratigraphy, structural geology, vulcanology and seismology. It **does not** include @hydrology and @soil science (pedology), which
 * are treated separately.
 */
ontology geology
	version 1.0
	using imod, physical, earth
	in domain imod:Geology;

/**
 * ## Geological material bodies
 *
 * RockBody identifies consolidated geological material as a bounded body.
 * SedimentBody identifies an accumulation of unconsolidated particles. A rock
 * specimen and a sand deposit can be observed through these material descriptions;
 * their rock types and grain-size classes describe different aspects.
 *
 * Boundaries follow stated material distinctions and delineation conventions.
 * A mapped outline alone does not identify the material body's depth or establish
 * that every disconnected occurrence is one body. Partial consolidation and
 * changing material call for observations of the particular body; the distinction
 * does not prescribe an exhaustive or disjoint classification.
 *
 * Material bodies can bear mass and volume observations without duplicating
 * those general qualities here. Economic use is a separate role, and a geological
 * body is distinct from the living communities associated with it.
 *
 * The material distinction follows the [USGS lithologic vocabulary](https://apps.usgs.gov/thesaurus/thesaurus-full.php?thcode=4).
 * Individuation and boundary conventions follow `physical:MaterialBody`.
 */
thing RockBody
    "An individuated body of consolidated geological material."
    is physical:MaterialBody;

thing SedimentBody
    "An individuated accumulation of unconsolidated geological particles."
    is physical:MaterialBody;

/**
 * ## Individual grains and particle size
 *
 * Grain identifies a discrete particle of mineral or rock material. It can be
 * described in soil or sediment without becoming a new material kind when its
 * observational setting changes. A grain can contain several minerals; it is
 * distinct from a mineral classification and from an accumulation of grains.
 *
 * GrainDiameter assigns a length to that individual under a stated convention.
 * An irregular particle has no unique diameter: an observed axis, a sieve-based
 * size and an equivalent diameter derived from settling need not agree. The
 * convention and preparation identify what is being measured.
 * Equal units do not make different diameter conventions interchangeable.
 * Observations state the geometric or equivalence criterion and preparation;
 * models must establish any conversion.
 *
 * A soil-body size distribution summarizes many particles on a common basis.
 * Its class fractions and median sizes are different observations from the
 * diameter of one grain. Their definitions state the included population and
 * weighting. The distinction follows [NRCS sediment properties](https://directives.nrcs.usda.gov/sites/default/files2/1720626005/Chapter%202%20-%20Sediment%20Properties.pdf),
 * sections on particle characteristics and size distributions.
 */
thing Grain
    "An individuated particle of mineral or rock material."
    is physical:MaterialBody;

quality GrainDiameter
    "Diameter assigned to an individual grain under a stated geometric or equivalent-size convention."
    is imod:Length of geology:Grain
    describes geology:Grain;

/**
 * ## Sediment movement and consolidation
 *
 * SedimentTransport moves geological particles; Lithification consolidates
 * sediment into coherent rock. Movement, deposition and consolidation describe
 * different processes. Transport can occur without deposition at the observed
 * site, and deposited sediment need not already be lithified.
 *
 * Geological material is distinct from its classification and any economic
 * resource role. Observations establish the material and its continuity through
 * movement or consolidation; recognizing a new rock body is a separate identity
 * judgment. Models describe transport, compaction and cementation in the
 * conditions of the particular occurrence.
 */
process SedimentTransport
    "Movement of unconsolidated geological particles.";

process Lithification
    "Consolidation of sediment into coherent rock.";

/**
 * ## Experimental first-level articulation
 *
 * These observations extend the shared vocabulary for multidisciplinary review.
 * Their declaration-local review records preserve rationale, alternatives and
 * exact validation evidence. Experimental inclusion is distinct from recorded
 * community acceptance; models still establish occurrence, support and outcomes.
 */

@review(
    schema="klab.review/1",
    proposal="wv-review-20261023-0061",
    revision="strawman-integration-r1",
    maturity="experimental",
    controversy="unassessed",
    technical_validation="not_recorded",
    community_acceptance="not_recorded",
    review_record="../docs/reviews/domain-integration-20261023/records/earth/geology.json"
)
structural bond MaterialContact
    "Observed physical contact between two geological material bodies under a stated boundary convention."
    is physical:PhysicalContact
    links (geology:RockBody or geology:SedimentBody) to (geology:RockBody or geology:SedimentBody);
